      {
        "id": "p57-q17-root",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{x+4}{3}+\\dfrac{x-2}{2}=5$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\dfrac{28}{5}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2(x+4)+3(x-2)}6&=5\\\\2x+8+3x-6&=30\\\\5x+2&=30\\\\5x&=28\\\\x&=\\frac{28}{5}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p57-q18-root",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac2{x+1}+\\dfrac3{x-2}=\\dfrac{10}{x^2-x-2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\dfrac{11}{5}$; original domain $x\\ne-1,2$.\n\nFactorise $x^2-x-2=(x+1)(x-2)$ and clear the common denominator.",
          "worked": "Since $x^2-x-2=(x+1)(x-2)$, the original domain is $x\\ne-1,2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2(x-2)+3(x+1)}{(x+1)(x-2)}&=\\frac{10}{(x+1)(x-2)}\\\\2(x-2)+3(x+1)&=10\\\\2x-4+3x+3&=10\\\\5x-1&=10\\\\5x&=11\\\\x&=\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{align*}$$\nThis value is in the original domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 86
      },
      {
        "id": "p57-q19-root",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$4x+9\\ge13$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x\\ge1$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}4x&\\ge4\\\\x&\\ge1.\\end{align*}$$\nDivision by positive $4$ preserves the inequality direction.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p57-q20-root",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$-2<3x-5\\le4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1<x\\le3$",
          "worked": "Add $5$ to all three expressions, then divide by positive $3$.\n$$\\begin{align*}3&<3x\\le9\\\\1&<x\\le3.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      }
    ]
